      "text": "The parameterization of the line is given by\n\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\pm t \\begin{pmatrix} 4/3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\end{pmatrix},\\]\n\nwhere the $\\pm$ symbol allows for the fact that any vector in the direction of the slope, scaled by $t$, will produce points on the line.\nIf we consider the $x$-value of the parameterization, $ \\frac{4}{3}t+3 $ will be $x \\ge 3$. This warns that the point that has a distance of $t$ from $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ in terms of $x$, must achieve this point. Since $x \\ge 3$, for this mentioned $x-values$, the $y$ value will be $1+t$ (which describes points of the line). \nThis prompts us to deduce that our points in $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} $ are $(3,1+t)$ and $(\\frac{4}{3}t+3,1+t)$. It then creates a right triangle of which $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ is the hypotenuse. This triangle has legs of length $t$ and $(\\frac{4}{3}t+3)-3 = \\frac{4}{3}t$, where $t \\ge 0$. So, $\\frac{4}{3}t^2 = \\left (\\frac{4}{3}t \\right)^ 2 + (1+t)^ 2$. After solving for $t$ (which must satisfy $\\frac{4}{3}t \\ge 1$) we can use/assign $\\frac{4}{3}t$ to our first attempt ($\\frac{4}{3}t=2$). Plugging it in, we can easily find our second attempt to be 1. Continue by setting $t_1=t_2$, which turns out to be $t = \\boxed {\\frac{4}{3}}$. The answer is \\frac{4}{3}",
