      "text": "We will use the parametric form with $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix},$ because when $x = 3,$ we also have $y = 1.$\n\nWhen $x \\ge 3,$ the distance between the two points is given by  \n\\[\\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + (y - 1)^2} = t.\\]Since $y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4},$ we get $\\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + \\frac{20(x - 3)}{16}} = t,$ or $\\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + \\frac{5(x - 3)^2}{4}} = t,$ or $\\sqrt{\\frac{9(x - 3)^2}{4}} = t,$ so $y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4} = t$ when $x \\ge 3 $ and $t \\ge 0.$\n\nLooking at this answer, we realize that we have to find the positive values of $t$ for which $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{v} + t \\mathbf{d}$ gives $t \\ge 0,$ so the step we need to take is to convert from the $t = 0$ and $t = 1$  boundary conditions to \n\\[ \\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + (1.5y + 1)^2} = t,\\]which gives $y = 0.75 \\frac{3x - 5}{4} + 1 = \\frac{3x}{4} - 0.25.$  Therefore, we want to solve for $\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\end{pmatrix}$ so that  \n\\[\\sqrt{\\left( \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\right) } = \\frac{\\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + \\left( \\frac{3x}{4} - 1.25 \\right)^2}}{4}.\\]This simplifies to  \n\\[\\sqrt{(x^2 - 6x + 9) + \\frac{9x^2 - 18x + 25}{16}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{(x^2 - 12x + 9) + \\left( \\frac{3x^2}{16} - \\frac{3x}{2} + 1\\right)}}{4}.\\]We can square a sum and get rid of the limits on inputs.  This gives us\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\  y \\end{pmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\  y \\end{pmatrix} , = \\frac{x^2 - 12x + 9 + \\frac{3x^2}{16} - \\frac{3x}{2} + 1}{16}.\\]Setting this equal to 16 and performing the multiplications on the left, we get \n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} x^2 \\\\   y^2     \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{\\frac{23}{16} x^2 - 19x + 18}{4}.\\]We can get rid of fractions on both sides by multiplying both sides by $16,$ which gives\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 16x^2 \\\\ 16y^2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{23x^2 - 304x + 144}{4}.\\]Next, we can multiply both sides by 4 so that the right side becomes $(4x^2 - 16)7.$  This gives us\n\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix}   16x^2 \\\\      16y^2     \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{23x^2 - 304x + 144}{4} = \\begin{pmatrix}   4x^2 - 16 \\\\       7 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Comparing coefficients, we get $16x^2 = 4x^2 - 16$ and $16y^2 = 7.$  Since $x \\ge 3,$ we only need to find the $\\mathbf{d}$ so that these equalities hold.  Therefore, we have $\\mathbf{d} = \\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0.5 \\\\ -0.375 \\end{pmatrix}}.$ The answer is \\begin{pmatrix}0.5\\-0.375\\end{pmatrix}",
